    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d661">

                <pb facs="1060" n="940" sameAs="frus:frus1932v01/pg940"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.801/522G</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Great Britain (Mellon)</hi><anchor n="17" xml:id="d661-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 14</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-04-25">April 25, 1932</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Having reference to your telegram No. 60,
                    dated February 11, 1932, concerning Congressional Bills H. R. 8874 and H. R.
                    8875, I wish to inform you that these bills have caused a number of protests
                    from European Governments, among these the British Government. The British
                    Ambassador at Washington called on February 15, 1932, and presented a note
                    containing the views of his Government. I enclose a copy of the note left at the
                    Department by Sir Ronald Lindsay.<anchor
                        n="18" xml:id="d661-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>The substance of this note, together with the substance of notes received from
                    other foreign representatives in Washington, was duly transmitted to the Senate
                    Committee on Commerce, the Chairman of which committee, the Honorable
                        Hiram Johnson, had requested comment <pb facs="1061"
                        n="941" xml:id="pg941"/> from the Department. A copy of the Secretary’s
                    letter of March 3, 1932, to Senator Johnson is
                        enclosed.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d661-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>I enclose also copies of H. R. 8874 and H. R. 8875.<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d661-f4"/> Identic bills were introduced in the Senate containing
                    the numbers 3501 and 3502.</p>
                <p>The bills in question have been dropped, but a substitute bill for H. R. 8874 has
                    been introduced, on March 19, 1932, in the House of Representatives under the
                    number H. R. 10674. A copy of this bill is enclosed.<anchor n="21"
                        xml:id="d661-f5"/> I understand that this bill is still pending in the House
                    of Representatives.</p>
                <p>I am informed that pressure by United States shipping interests for the passage
                    of this or similar legislation will again be increased since the negotiations
                    designed to reach a compromise with foreign shipping interests seem to have
                    become deadlocked. It is, however, possible that an agreement may still
                    ultimately be reached. In this connection I enclose a copy of the memorandum of
                    conversation between Mr. Bundy and the
                    British Ambassador held on March 30, 1932.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d661-f6"/>
                </p>
                <p>I propose to keep you informed of developments in this matter and should be glad
                    if you will let me have any information which may reach you on the attitude of
                    the Cunard and other interests in Great Britain.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. R. Castle, Jr.</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
